a Δx=0.1$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=0.8$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=5$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method \n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.1)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.1}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.1}| \\\\  & =0.9090…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.8)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.8}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.8}| \\\\  & =0.5555…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.2)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.2}| \\\\  & =0.4545…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(5)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+5}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{6}| \\\\  & =0.1666…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\".\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f45ad8ed-5b6d-5e46-b337-02ab271013b3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections From Functions Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5d145da-97ce-547e-a753-1a83cee2cff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385614} {:answer/id #uuid "cd76936e-f041-580d-8d47-910ac26ba515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385615} {:answer/id #uuid "32e81036-11a9-565b-bcd3-4fe2fe9eb20e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385616} {:answer/id #uuid "956ffac4-21fd-51f0-b89d-d174fd85de32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385617} {:answer/id #uuid "97a98d26-73da-54aa-be15-fe6ff504cf76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "956ffac4-21fd-51f0-b89d-d174fd85de32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385617} :answer-field/id #uuid "762e03d4-09ef-50a3-abcb-d5091d662911" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7c69759b-6bd0-5ee4-a581-0fdeb88d4d4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-76241" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\n- $g:R\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{4}$\n- $h:[0,2\\pi ]\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- $f:Q\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is *not*  an injection.  For example, $g(1)=1=g(-1),$ but $1\\ne -1.$\n\n- II is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=1=h(2\\pi ),$ but $0\\ne 2\\pi .$\n\n- III is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{{x}_{1}}) & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{{x}_{2}}) \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f45ad8ed-5b6d-5e46-b337-02ab271013b3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections From Functions Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d476250b-936b-59a4-989c-bb04ac46d79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385640} {:answer/id #uuid "88ec0c4c-63d8-5a82-acd3-403167b0c8e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385641} {:answer/id #uuid "8cac40e1-6aa5-5a30-a598-266d6e10f1b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385642} {:answer/id #uuid "9a90c907-263f-5c92-a65b-18d4116ec2f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385643} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f757fe-bf63-55b0-9c6b-666d2da5b195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cac40e1-6aa5-5a30-a598-266d6e10f1b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385642} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c51726e-f69d-556c-9121-188dd98391fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03ec5353-d045-5242-94cf-0882c6a84756" :question/math-academy-id "q-77455" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\n- $f:{1,2,3,4}\\to {1,2,3,4,5,6,7}$ defined by $f(x)=x+1$\n- $g:{-4,-2,0,2,4,8,10}\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$\n- $h:Z\\to {-1,0,1}$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}+1 & ={x}_{2}+1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- II is *not* an injection. For example, $g(-4)=16=g(4),$ but $-4\\ne 4.$\n\n- III is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=0=h(1),$ but $0\\ne 1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f45ad8ed-5b6d-5e46-b337-02ab271013b3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections From Functions Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5273ce7-e84c-543e-bd20-674a98e65b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385647} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e84721-013c-5dab-a69e-82c411cf0006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385648} {:answer/id #uuid "5d31af35-5d65-5adf-9858-1bc955c652dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385649} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7fbe82-61d9-536f-8d91-395851ee6150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385650} {:answer/id #uuid "e723d2d6-4c15-54b6-81b2-89fc8c381dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e723d2d6-4c15-54b6-81b2-89fc8c381dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385651} :answer-field/id #uuid "e31cc959-bec9-528e-b368-de6a5467600d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "75f6f36b-64d5-52cc-809a-ed8ce71cf663" :question/math-academy-id "q-77463" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\n- $f:{1,3,5,7,9}\\to {-4,-2,0,12}$ defined by $f(x)=(x-3)(x-7)$\n- $g:{1,2,3,4,5}\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $h:Z\\to {-1,1}$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is *not* an injection. For example, $f(3)=0=f(7),$ but $3\\ne 7.$\n\n- II is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- III is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=1=h(2),$ but $0\\ne 2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f45ad8ed-5b6d-5e46-b337-02ab271013b3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections From Functions Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fdb96f4-2021-5751-80da-5b36eba1c5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385654} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cf459e-1c5f-55aa-b32e-e6f27d973712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385655} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1fe5e0-d060-5bf7-b7b8-af59cd760672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385656} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb7581c-d0d2-537a-a6f7-c3e316402841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385657} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7583c0-458f-586c-b7b4-f4f7d74d0eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bb7581c-d0d2-537a-a6f7-c3e316402841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385657} :answer-field/id #uuid "da13ca19-0d2d-5bd1-810f-7ea3a2872fc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3065c9f8-73e6-55e4-8dcd-b18452c2889c" :question/math-academy-id "q-77465" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\n- $f:{-1,0,1,2,3}\\to Z$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{3}$\n- $g:{-2,-1,0,1,2}\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={2}^{x}$\n- $h:Z\\to {-1,1}$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}^{3} & ={x}_{2}^{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- II is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ {2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- III is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=1=h(2),$ but $0\\ne 2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152265 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f237343d-2f8a-5a9f-9e5f-c9f22784547a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Functions Defined Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37687926-8f0a-55b2-83ff-19bf952a848e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385661} {:answer/id #uuid "43bdea7f-ce92-58be-a635-2f4a1e204def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385662} {:answer/id #uuid "595dcb01-bbd6-5210-ab29-62ed9755bcf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385663} {:answer/id #uuid "d09d989e-e4f6-5f62-a325-c1ab71cb28fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385664} {:answer/id #uuid "60d3f75f-6de2-582c-b480-08e1c57e99b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60d3f75f-6de2-582c-b480-08e1c57e99b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385665} :answer-field/id #uuid "06fb7149-2f4c-5ed2-99b2-de2082bfaad8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "93abda0d-dd4f-575f-bfb4-2c2551c6ca10" :question/math-academy-id "q-77821" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n\n- $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=|x|$\n- $g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)=4(x-2)$\n- $h:Q\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. For example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-1.$\n\n- II is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in Q,$ we can choose an $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =y \\\\ 4(x-2) & =y \\\\ x & =\\frac{y}{4}+2\\in Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that, \n\n$$\ng(x)=g(\\frac{y}{4}+2)=4([\\frac{y}{4}+2]-2)=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ 4({x}_{1}-2) & =4({x}_{2}-2) \\\\ {x}_{1}-2 & ={x}_{2}-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $g$ is both surjective and injective, $g$ is bijective.\n\n- III is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is $[-1,1].$ This means that \n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo,  for example, there is no value $x\\in Q$ such that $h(x)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb4d348b-1703-51a1-be05-2e66295cacaa" :knowledge-point/title "Completing Trivial Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ed1627e-3a9b-545f-a470-f70f3b412422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385668} {:answer/id #uuid "961a0f6b-ba1a-5a8a-ab23-6a9b3c7a685e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385669} {:answer/id #uuid "b8284321-e3c3-5cab-b1f5-2b86d60a89aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385670} {:answer/id #uuid "811cd301-33d8-55d2-9cba-e1a1280a5c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385671} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ae5864-50c4-57c3-99cf-642bad9d32d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8284321-e3c3-5cab-b1f5-2b86d60a89aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385670} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbc8c3ae-0777-508b-b857-bfead8e728a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6066c3ca-d4e8-51fc-956b-cd9926b46fbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-80083" :question/problem "Let $n\\in Z.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $2$ is even, then $______.$\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is trivial?\n\n- $3$ is odd\n- $4$ is odd\n- $n$ is odd" :question/worked-solution "We say that an implication has a *trivial proof* if the consequent is always true:\n\n$$\nP\\,⇒\\overset{\\text{Always true}}{\\overset{⏞}{Q}}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our consequents in turn.\n\n- Statement I is always true. $\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is always false. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is *not* always true. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152194 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c805415f-c87d-50c5-b668-948fc46bcc7e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0c3f959-5171-59ff-838d-51e3fe713293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385687} {:answer/id #uuid "a30df9d4-c782-52c3-84d6-c86e15e7d455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385688} {:answer/id #uuid "04338b5f-5888-5616-8ee6-13a24f4daad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385689} {:answer/id #uuid "430f4a78-64b1-52ba-a7c6-ccdf11ed801d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385690} {:answer/id #uuid "ae89a223-8688-5ddc-8a2f-de64b6ac2023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0c3f959-5171-59ff-838d-51e3fe713293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385687} :answer-field/id #uuid "df761ac9-90f4-5c6f-ba68-ad1faf9f4e22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4333b20-5791-5886-a0e1-73362bfffb70" :question/math-academy-id "q-80365" :question/problem "![](images/c3/c3caf194f47cbc3fe36f969442df48e0a133ddbf77a9177e2e5e96822463c8a4.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(0)\\ne f(1)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is an injection" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the diagram, we have $f(0)=9$ (we have an arrow $0\\in X$ to $9\\in Y$) and $f(1)=0$ (we have an arrow from $1\\in X$ to $0\\in Y$).\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152194 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c805415f-c87d-50c5-b668-948fc46bcc7e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355cb717-f2f7-5938-8e6d-fccd5ad1370b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385694} {:answer/id #uuid "04266d4e-24fd-5e94-be7c-9ac7f473a6fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385695} {:answer/id #uuid "54b387d6-9737-5f35-be3e-eea0e72ba83e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385696} {:answer/id #uuid "903c5ed8-6223-5d82-8334-35b36775775e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385697} {:answer/id #uuid "acdbd104-53bf-5b27-9c70-ebe287beff57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "355cb717-f2f7-5938-8e6d-fccd5ad1370b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385694} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ed99050-40cd-578a-8860-47f97fead64c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf90a3c5-cc95-537c-9537-397ca87cb6b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-80368" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e187742134e111e2f539fdce556c50445cc1ee8d8c6cdb93077192c14b36dfaf.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(-5)\\ne f(0)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is **one-to-one**" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the diagram, we have $f(-5)=3$ (we have an arrow from $-5\\in X$ to $3\\in Y$) and $f(0)=-5$ (we have an arrow from $0\\in X$ to $-5\\in Y$).\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152196 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a347133c-5e5a-5dd2-89ef-bdd14245ee6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answ