o known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the graph, we have $f(-1)=f(1)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Again, we have $f(-1)=f(1)$ but $-1\\ne 1.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $R,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152196 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a347133c-5e5a-5dd2-89ef-bdd14245ee6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96ffb7a3-e0c9-5b3d-b5d4-f5aba8e22d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385708} {:answer/id #uuid "4eae2857-be44-5148-a73c-dc3aa950ed44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385709} {:answer/id #uuid "39096522-7d0f-5710-a2a0-0806c8df2a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385710} {:answer/id #uuid "cedf6393-d2e5-53cd-9c60-b420c1e586aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385711} {:answer/id #uuid "151e8bdb-a031-5ed4-befa-ca0f4c6ff1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96ffb7a3-e0c9-5b3d-b5d4-f5aba8e22d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385708} :answer-field/id #uuid "2289c275-f115-5326-8e97-b8a4a13e9e8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb0a5436-5069-574d-ac30-ea6987db593d" :question/math-academy-id "q-80376" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2d81a4317d789968de29f34b42358d44e74c8bbe4717467a4c780beb633dc0d5.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- $f(-2)\\ne f(1)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is **one-to-one**" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the graph, we have $f(-2)=f(1)=-1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Again, we have $f(-2)=f(1)$ but $-2\\ne 1.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $R,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99c2db8c-670b-521b-8942-3b89f1e95d3f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Whose Domain or Codomain Are Real Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e2e3bd4-c605-5900-89a7-c42b04f70825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385729} {:answer/id #uuid "65ba502e-895c-5928-81f8-eda852b62fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385730} {:answer/id #uuid "94735337-c07e-5e57-a4ea-21e21bec7fc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385731} {:answer/id #uuid "39c845c9-a1cc-5970-ac2c-329b8395e9c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385732} {:answer/id #uuid "5eae2762-adfc-5bd6-b11b-583b65dbb707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65ba502e-895c-5928-81f8-eda852b62fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385730} :answer-field/id #uuid "e26db430-4903-5213-a3cd-614fd0d112cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5a04c07f-9732-5fba-9678-462e7c23fd4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-80387" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[1,\\infty )\\to [0,\\infty )$ which is given by $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}?$\n\n- $f(1)\\ne f(2)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in [1,\\infty ),$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is **one-to-one**" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/ae/aeda30e69b9bc433e5f345822d4221e194981ce132a022f28632814376c5910e.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(1)=(1-1)^{2}=0$ and $f(2)=(2-1)^{2}=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $[1,\\infty )$ that map to the same element in $[0,\\infty ).$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $[1,\\infty ),$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152262 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22bcdfb2-485a-5242-a5b5-dd9ef8cee027" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22c06214-2610-5c0b-af4c-76319cd294f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385770} {:answer/id #uuid "9c4c7672-15c0-508f-99b1-e2ad662ac84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385771} {:answer/id #uuid "f141b7df-d2b4-5a9a-b43c-9d096a0472a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385772} {:answer/id #uuid "32cbbca9-9f7c-5c18-9146-8abe005299c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385773} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf06603-aa20-57d2-b5ae-904b39a1373a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bf06603-aa20-57d2-b5ae-904b39a1373a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385774} :answer-field/id #uuid "9179f998-6f44-58a9-800b-79c989241d78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e966053-1b79-5d5f-9b9a-6962c445b14d" :question/math-academy-id "q-82293" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd6de23e9b33d0e86a91eb4bf6b81a9d443c1b389182f1c05bf10d048f0c0402.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f$ is a surjection\n- $f$ is an injection\n- $f$ is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Consider $y=5.$ As we can see from the diagram, there are no elements contained within $X$ that have an arrow to $5\\in Y.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $Y,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=y.$ As a result, $f$ is *not* a surjection.\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ As a result, $f$ is an injection.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement I is false,  $f$ is not a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152262 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22bcdfb2-485a-5242-a5b5-dd9ef8cee027" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c4908f3-1701-512c-917e-6ac6928983cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385777} {:answer/id #uuid "b64b5d1c-6ecb-5dac-93b2-900d09943de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385778} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ea745f-1def-5066-9449-27c1819df19c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385779} {:answer/id #uuid "8809d663-38fa-5805-a29e-a896c62ade4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385780} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb720ca-da9b-5c5c-8fcf-34d156601d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b64b5d1c-6ecb-5dac-93b2-900d09943de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385778} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea10d5ad-68c8-5c6c-bfdd-d759d3732695" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3819ae9-243b-5030-9bcc-f473557d3d03" :question/math-academy-id "q-82297" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bbe19e6a595d7b48e7f18382ed0a66452ba28c86925edad81a296cb7dd26a77f.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f$ is a surjection\n- $f$ is an injection\n- $f$ is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, according to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$ So, $f$ is a surjection.\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ As a result, $f$ is an injection.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since both statements I and II are true, $f$ is a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152262 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22bcdfb2-485a-5242-a5b5-dd9ef8cee027" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fc68439-4269-5143-aa3a-d